review['thumbnail']['analysis']='The authentic 1280×720 image uses a white background and huge black text, DEBT MONEY / RULE. A cartoon man in a hoodie points toward a lower sequence of cash and a document, a house, a shop emitting banknotes, and a bank. Red curved arrows connect the steps; a suited hand drops a gold coin into the bank. Green cash, blue bank windows and thick black outlines give the small illustrations separation. At 320×180 the headline, character and broad asset-flow sequence remain readable; document details are too small to read. The title promises six methods while the image promises one money system, rather than a literal six-item chart. The flow omits liabilities and costs, which can make gross asset accumulation appear equivalent to wealth. Current image identity is verified; historical artwork, CTR and causal performance are unknown.'
review['script']['analysis']='A fictional Diego-versus-Curtis contrast opens with wealth apparently rising when one man borrows, while a debt-free neighbor stagnates. A good/bad debt binary introduces five mechanisms and a sixth other-peoples-money synthesis. Rental leverage and business acquisition include early loss and failure warnings; the portfolio section warns about maintenance calls, and the closing explicitly discusses survivorship bias and four conditions. Those cautions matter. However, free-money, cheat-code and taxman-never-gets-his-slice language can obscure the conditions. A community pitch interrupts between methods five and six and returns at the end. The rental 25% is gross appreciation divided by equity, not net total return. Narrated 340K less 166K equals 174K; the growth path and loan terms are unspecified. Business earnings are undefined. One-time full-sale tax and recurring interest on a smaller loan have different cash needs and horizons. The tax section misses an existing qualified vehicle-interest exception. Closing captions say 150K down versus 50K earlier; audio is unverified. The 39 claim groups, 20 source receipts and 15 calculations separate correct equations, conditional illustrations, unsupported statistics and text inconsistencies. These are fictional protagonists, not a measured causal comparison. This review observes text structure, not delivery, editing, music or retention.'
review['topic']['analysis']='The topic is productive leverage and conditional wealth creation. Valid mechanisms include after-cost asset returns, principal amortization funded by actual operating income, eligible tax deductions and deferral, and erosion of fixed nominal debt through inflation. Borrowing itself creates equal assets and liabilities; it does not create profit. Gross assets, equity, liquidity and spendable income must be kept distinct. The video includes important downside warnings but leaves several examples without complete costs, matched cash needs or eligibility conditions. No HELOC, credit-card promotion or four-percent withdrawal example appears in the transcript, so none is imported into this audit.'
review['virality']['analysis']=f'At the frozen snapshot, 4,462 views over 65.1336 days equals {F["target_vpd"]:.8f} views/day. The ten-peer median is {F["median_10"]:.8f}, giving {F["ratio_10"]:.5f} TYPICAL; the five-peer median is {F["median_5"]:.8f}, giving {F["ratio_5"]:.5f} TYPICAL. The tier is stable across these windows. This does not establish poor demand for debt content or a packaging failure. The contrarian wealth promise, numbered structure and money-flow image plausibly attract interest, but their contribution cannot be identified from public counts.'
review['topic']['novelty_and_corpus_angle']='An original explanation could follow one borrower through six linked balance-sheet panels: asset, outstanding loan, operating cash, interest and principal, tax, and net equity. Keep cash needs and horizons consistent, display base and adverse cases, and distinguish independent strategies from tax attributes that overlap with the same investment. Show required cash reserves rather than promising inevitable wealth.'
